in america's deep south  a stretch of the mississippi with its  own notorious nickname we call this  cancer alley  where poor black communities say they're  being poisoned by mile after mile of  heavy industry  we are nothing we're not people that  live here  now plans for a huge new plastics plant  threatened the skies over louisiana  and the people are fighting back i'm  gonna be here  to fight till i die to make sure that  another plant don't come here but can  they take on big industry  and win  over the mississippi and into cancer  alley as the people who live here call  it  where the billboards say it all and  where for mile after mile  petrochemical plants belch out fumes and  pollution  wedged between them poor largely black  communities  trapped unable to move and they say  becoming  increasingly sick it's hard for living  here  you know knowing that uh you won't make  it to be old age  you know like our ancestors that we they  live to be  90 95 and now you have people dying at  36 on up you know  even younger  you know the kids is our tomorrow and  with what's going on now we're worried  about the kids  uh getting cancer  [Music]  the houses and chemical plants are in  amongst each other  people living under the shadow of their  industrial neighbors  in one of the most heavily polluted  areas in america  i'm so hurt you know every day is a cry  and now we have the covet on top of  cancer  it is it's you don't even want to leave  out your house in the morning  and it smells y'all just don't know how  we have a smell  comes here when those plants are  escaping  gases and all the stuff they're letting  out  janna's first show is typical of most  people you talk to here  they either have cancer or know many  others who do and they blame it on the  pollution  it's me myself my brother  my sister i have uh about  eight cousins and my neighbor  uh and it's it's a quite bit more but  at least for sure in this area where i'm  at is 20.  all have cancer who has cancer i'm just  happy that the lord took it away from my  body  but i got a little cousin she stay in  the project  this child is in her 30s and  she had to have both of her breasts  removed my sister had to have both of  her breasts  and that chemo is just tearing them down  a new un report has condemned what's  happening in cancer alley  as environmental racism a legacy of  slavery  the industrial plants are built on  plantation land  and many in the poor black communities  who live work and worship here  are descendants of slaves still getting  a raw deal  they're the weakest people they're the  poorest people and they  often not the most educated people so we  put the plant there  you know and the plantation mentality  has been here  many of these plants are located on all  plantations  so this goes back a long way it goes  back a long way  a long way and it's very disappointing  but it's time to turn it around  but far from turning it round the state  has given permission  for the biggest plant yet it goes from  that  from that pole over there where you see  all the electric crackers they did the  electrical work  lead from there all the way to the blue  sign down here and all it's going to be  for mosul  and as with this plant coming  and that plant right there we're on a  time bomb  taiwanese company foremost of plastics  plans one of the biggest plastic plants  in the world here  covering two and a half thousand acres  but not without a fight  from local people i have a list of  people  that have already died from cancer then  i have a list  of people who are dying with cancer as  we speak  i feel like our turn is coming i know  there's something  harsh to say but in my heart i feel like  that  fed up with watching friends dying from  cancer high school teacher sharon levine  has mobilized her community against the  formosa plant  i feel bad inside because it's a it's a  death sentence  i feel like it's a death sentence and i  feel like  if mimosa comes here they want us to die  off  they want us to just pack up and leave  the people don't have the funds to  pack up and leave and go somewhere else  i don't have the funds either  so they they're not offering to buy  anyone out they just plan to come here  and just  build and put their filth on us so  they don't care about the people they  don't care about human lives  and how much are you prepared to fight  that i'm gonna fight to the  end yes indeed that plant gonna be two  miles from my house  they must be losing their mind god put  the right person here to fight  god put the fight in me this is my home  this is my people and i'm gonna fight  for me and my people  but it's not just cancer-causing  chemicals from the proposed plant  that are causing concern if it's built  it's estimated the new plastics plant  will be  pumping into the atmosphere something  like two and a half million  cars worth of carbon a year making it  one of the biggest single sources of  carbon in america  and contributing to something already  causing problems down in the mississippi  delta  climate change  we took to the waters on an airboat to  see the impact climate change is having  on louisiana's coastline diggers are  trying to claw back marshland beyond the  levees  lost to rising water levels this  excavator is lifting the mud out of this  borough ditch  hampering the work an unprecedented five  hurricanes have swept through in the  last year  blamed by many scientists on climate  change it's like they all come in one  it was bam bam bam you know like one  came we got out of here we came back  another one came we got out here we came  back another one came we got out here we  came back it was just  repetitious over and over further down  the coast is a place called phoenix  ravaged not by fire but by those five  hurricanes in the last year  showing little respect for anything in  their path  it's a worry when you leave you don't  know if you're coming home to anything  it's hard i mean it's hard to  do four or five times a year  i know the hurricanes they're getting  worse they're getting worse and they're  getting more frequent  i'm sure it's because of things that's  happening around the world  the wind was so strong with the tornado  type wind that we had it just  kenny bartellomy also watched his  neighbor's trailer get trashed  by storms he says that are only getting  worse  it just seemed like they're getting more  frequently  and powerful as time go on  and just like every time they come it  just seems like it's more and more  more and more powerful more and more  damage so people  watching might think why do you live  here kenny why do you live in a place  that's hit by hurricanes so often  you know i just feel like you can't run  from god's work  so just gotta deal with it  in a statement to sky news formosa  plastics insisted its new plant  meets all regulatory criteria using  advanced emissions reduction mechanisms  and extensive measures to protect the  environment the state of louisiana says  great care is taken in the site  selection process to identify locations  that safeguard communities and their  residents state and federal regulators  require an extensive environmental  permitting process before construction  can begin  and they say plans for new plants are  subject to public consultation  but america's new president joe biden  has singled out  cancer alley by name as somewhere  environmental justice  needs to be addressed the people who  live there  say they'll hold him to his word it's  time to put a stop to the plants  and even before they start breaking  ground it's time to stop  enough is enough how much more can we  stand  we dying slowly they're killing us  slowly  and my question to everyone  why are you making our area a sacrifice  zone dominic waghorn  sky news louisiana

